  "findings": {
    "scaling": "Aggregate throughput scales near-linearly to K=32 while wall clock stays almost flat: K=1 28.7 tok/s @ 8.92s -> K=32 739.9 tok/s @ 10.75s. 25.8x the tokens for 1.21x the wall clock.",
    "knee": "K=32. K=48 still gains but wall clock rises to 13.62s; K=64 COLLAPSES to 512.6 tok/s @ 29.59s.",
    "why_it_collapses": "K=64 exceeds the server's own declared max concurrency of 61.83x, so requests preempt instead of batching. The measured cliff sits where the KV-cache arithmetic says it should -- this is predictable per GPU, not a magic constant.",
    "deployment_rule": "Choose K below floor(kv_cache_tokens / max_model_len). Here 506544/8192 = 61.8, and 64 broke. Read it from vLLM's own boot log.",
    "prefix_cache": "Verified search issues K prompts that are IDENTICAL except for sampling temperature. Measured hit rate 99.51% vs 1.35% for K distinct prompts at K=16, worth 410.9 vs 292.9 tok/s (1.40x) at 9.97s vs 13.98s (0.71x). The agent's access pattern is itself a GPU optimization.",
    "correctness_consequence": "P(correct) = 1-(1-p)^K. At p=0.3: K=6 -> 88.2%, K=16 -> 99.7%, K=32 -> 99.999%. On this hardware K=32 costs 1.21x wall clock."
